    'modules' => [
        [
            'title' => 'What "Late Talking" Actually Means',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => "<p>Every child develops language at their own pace, but there are well-established milestones that help us tell typical variation from a genuine delay. Most toddlers say their first words around 12 months, have roughly 10-20 words by 18 months, and are combining two words together (like \"more juice\" or \"mama up\") by 24 months. A child who has fewer than 10 words at 18 months, or isn't combining words by 24 months, is generally described as a \"late talker.\"</p><p>Being a late talker doesn't automatically mean something is wrong. Some late talkers catch up on their own, especially if they understand language well, use gestures to communicate, and are social with others. But because it's hard to know in advance which toddlers will catch up and which will need support, the safest approach is never to \"wait and see\" indefinitely.</p><p>This module is about building accurate expectations, not alarm. Knowing the real milestones means you can act early if needed, and relax if your child is genuinely on track.</p>"]],
