        [
            'title' => 'Everyday Techniques That Help',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => "<p>You don't need special materials or a therapy room to help your toddler talk more. Three simple techniques, used during ordinary moments like snack time, bath time, or play, can make a real difference.</p><p><strong>Wait time</strong> means pausing after you ask a question or offer a choice, giving your child a full 5-10 seconds to respond before you jump in. It feels like a long time, but toddlers often need that space to process and attempt a word. <strong>Parallel talk</strong> means narrating what your child is doing in simple language as they do it (\"you're pushing the car... fast car... car goes!\") rather than asking a stream of questions. <strong>Expansion</strong> means taking whatever your child says and adding one more piece: if they say \"ball,\" you say \"big ball\" or \"ball bounces.\"</p><p>None of these require your child to repeat after you. The goal is rich exposure to language connected to things they're already interested in, which is how children actually learn words.</p>"]],
                ['type' => 'practical_quiz', 'content' => ['questions' => [
                    ['question' => 'Your toddler points at a cup and grunts. What is the most helpful response?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Say "cup! you want your cup" and hand it over', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Ask "what do you want?" repeatedly until they say a word', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Ignore the grunt until they say "cup" clearly', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Hand over the cup without saying anything', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Naming what they want while meeting the need pairs the word with its meaning in a low-pressure way, which supports word learning better than demanding speech or saying nothing.'],
                    ['question' => 'What is "wait time" in toddler communication?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Pausing 5-10 seconds after speaking to give the child a chance to respond', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Waiting a few months before worrying about language', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Letting the child watch TV while you do chores', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Delaying meals until the child asks for food', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Wait time is a deliberate pause that gives a toddler the processing time they need to attempt a word or gesture, rather than an adult immediately filling the silence.'],
                    ['question' => 'What does "expansion" mean when talking with a late-talking toddler?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Adding one extra word to what the child just said', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Correcting the child\'s pronunciation immediately', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Asking the child to repeat a longer sentence', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Teaching new vocabulary unrelated to the moment', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Expansion builds naturally on the child\'s own words and interest, modeling slightly more complex language without pressure to imitate.'],
                ]]],
            ],
