        [
            'title' => 'A Family Story: Noticing and Acting',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'case_study', 'content' => ['body' => "<p>When their son Omar turned 18 months, Hina and her husband noticed he had only about six words and relied heavily on pointing and pulling their hands to get what he wanted. Relatives reassured them that \"boys talk later\" and that his older cousin had been \"a late bloomer too.\" Hina appreciated the reassurance but kept a mental note of his words over the next two months, and the count barely moved.</p><p>At his 20-month check-up, Hina mentioned this to their pediatrician, who referred them for a speech-language evaluation rather than suggesting they wait further. The SLP assessed Omar's understanding of language, his play skills, and his sound and word use, and recommended a short course of parent-coached sessions focused on wait time and expansion, along with rechecking progress in a few months.</p><p>Hina later said the hardest part wasn't the therapy, it was pushing past the well-meaning advice to \"just wait.\" Omar's word count began climbing within weeks of the family changing how they talked with him day to day.</p>", 'links' => [['url' => 'https://alzahra.institute/resources', 'reference' => 'Al Zahra Institute Resource Library']]]],
                ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => "Have you ever received reassurance from family that turned out to not match what you were seeing in your child? How did you decide whether to act on your own observations?"]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'When and How to Seek an Evaluation',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => "<p>Seeking a speech-language evaluation is not an overreaction, and it does not commit your child to years of therapy. It's simply a professional check, much like a vision or hearing test, that gives you clear information. You should consider requesting one if your child has fewer than 10 words at 18 months, isn't combining two words by 24 months, seems to understand very little of what's said to them, rarely tries to imitate sounds or words, or has lost words or skills they previously had (which is always worth checking regardless of age).</p><p>You can ask your pediatrician for a referral, or in many areas you can contact a speech-language pathologist directly without one. An evaluation usually involves play-based observation, a review of your child's history, and sometimes a standardized assessment of understanding and expression. You'll get specific, actionable feedback either way: either reassurance with things to watch, or a clear plan.</p><p>Early support, when needed, tends to be shorter and more effective than support started later, which is why acting on genuine concerns sooner rather than later matters.</p>"]],
