    'modules' => [
        [
            'title' => 'Debunking the Myths',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => "<p>One of the most persistent myths parents hear is that raising a child with two or more languages will \"confuse\" them or delay their speech. This isn't supported by the research. Bilingual children follow the same general milestones as monolingual children, such as first words around 12 months and simple sentences by age two to three, when you count their total vocabulary across both languages rather than expecting each language alone to match a monolingual child's.</p><p>It's true that a bilingual child's vocabulary in any single language may look smaller at a given moment than a monolingual peer's vocabulary in that one language. This is a normal distribution of word-learning across two systems, not a deficit. When you add up words known in both languages, bilingual children are typically right where you'd expect.</p><p>Bilingualism is also linked to cognitive benefits such as flexible attention-switching, and it carries deep value for family connection, culture, and identity. The old advice to \"just pick one language\" was never backed by evidence, and dropping a home language has real, lasting costs for family relationships.</p>"]],
                ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => "What language-related advice have you been given by family, friends, or professionals? Did it match what you've read here, and how did it affect your own decisions?"]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Choosing an Approach That Fits Your Family',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => "<p>There is no single correct way to raise a bilingual child, and the best approach is the one your family can sustain consistently. <strong>One-parent-one-language</strong> means each parent consistently speaks their own language to the child, which gives clear, strong exposure to each language but requires both parents to stick with it even when it feels unnatural, like at a mixed gathering.</p><p><strong>Time-and-place</strong> means designating when or where each language is used, such as one language at home and another at school or with grandparents, which works well when a community language needs extra reinforcement. <strong>Mixed-language</strong> households, where both parents use both languages depending on context, are common and perfectly workable too, especially in multilingual communities, as long as the child gets substantial, regular exposure to each language rather than just occasional words.</p><p>What matters most across all approaches is quantity and quality of real interaction in each language, not rigid rule-following. A relaxed, consistent approach beats a strict one you abandon after a few weeks.</p>"]],
