                    ['question' => 'Which approach involves each parent consistently using their own native language with the child?', 'options' => [['text' => 'One-parent-one-language', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Time-and-place', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Code-switching', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Silent period', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'One-parent-one-language is defined by each parent sticking to one consistent language, giving the child clear, strong exposure to both.'],
                    ['question' => 'What matters most for successful bilingual development, regardless of which approach a family chooses?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Consistent, substantial, quality interaction in each language', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Starting the second language only after age 5', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Using flashcards for vocabulary drills daily', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Never mixing the two languages in the same sentence', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Across all approaches, what predicts strong bilingual outcomes is the amount and quality of genuine interaction a child gets in each language, not strict separation rules.'],
                ]]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Code-Switching Is a Skill, Not a Problem',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'research_paper', 'content' => ['body' => "<p>Tariq and his wife raise their three children speaking Urdu at home and English outside, following a fairly natural time-and-place pattern. When their eldest, age four, started mixing languages mid-sentence, saying things like \"mujhe that one chahiye,\" Tariq worried she was mixing the languages up because she didn't fully know either one.</p><p>A conversation with a speech-language pathologist at a community workshop reassured him that this kind of mixing, called code-switching, is something bilingual children (and adults) do naturally and skillfully. It usually reflects reaching for the word that comes to mind first or fits the moment best, not confusion. True bilingual fluency often includes fluid movement between languages, sometimes within the same sentence, especially among family members who share both languages.</p><p>Tariq stopped correcting the mixing and instead focused on making sure his daughter got rich, full sentences in both languages across the week. Her mixing gradually became more context-driven as her vocabulary in each language grew, exactly the pattern researchers describe as typical bilingual development.</p>", 'links' => [['url' => 'https://alzahra.institute/resources', 'reference' => 'Al Zahra Institute Resource Library']]]],
