[
    'title' => "Understanding Your Child's Stammer",
    'audience' => 'parents',
    'level' => 'beginner',
    'short_desc' => 'Helps parents tell normal toddler disfluency apart from true stuttering, and offers evidence-based ways to respond that reduce pressure and support fluent speech.',
    'outcomes' => [
        'Distinguish typical developmental disfluency from signs of true stuttering',
        'Apply practical strategies like slowing your own speech and reducing time pressure during conversation',
        'Know when stuttering warrants a speech-language pathology referral',
    ],
    'modules' => [
        [
            'title' => 'Normal Bumps vs. True Stuttering',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => "<p>Almost all young children go through a phase of disfluent speech, usually between ages two and five, as their language and motor planning skills race to keep up with the complex sentences they're starting to form. This typical disfluency sounds like whole-word or phrase repetitions (\"I want, I want, I want juice\"), and revisions (\"he went- she went to the store\"), used easily and without visible struggle.</p><p>True stuttering looks and sounds different. It often includes repeating parts of words rather than whole words (\"b-b-ball\"), stretching sounds out (\"ssssee\"), or getting stuck with no sound coming out at all. It may be accompanied by visible tension, such as eye blinking, head movement, or a change in pitch or volume as the child pushes through the block. Stuttering can also come with the child showing awareness or frustration, like avoiding certain words or saying \"I can't say it.\"</p><p>Many children stutter for a period and recover naturally, especially within the first 12 months of onset. But because some children do not recover on their own, tracking what you're seeing matters more than guessing.</p>"]],
                ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => "Think about the specific sounds and behaviors your child shows when they get \"stuck.\" Does what you're noticing sound closer to typical disfluency or true stuttering as described here?"]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'How You Respond Matters',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => "<p>Parents often worry that anything they do might make stuttering worse, which can lead to either anxious over-correction or avoiding the topic altogether. Neither is necessary. A few well-established habits genuinely help reduce the pressure that can worsen stuttering, without requiring you to become a speech therapist.</p><p>Slow down your own rate of speech slightly and build in natural pauses; children often unconsciously match the pace of people around them. Resist finishing your child's sentences or supplying the word they're reaching for, even though it's tempting, since this can increase their sense of time pressure and reduce confidence in trying again. Keep eye contact relaxed and patient while they talk, rather than looking away or looking worried, and respond to the content of what they said rather than how it was said.</p><p>Reduce overall time pressure in daily life where you can, such as allowing a few extra minutes before leaving the house or during mealtime conversation, since rushed, high-demand moments tend to increase disfluency for many children.</p>"]],
                ['type' => 'practical_quiz', 'content' => ['questions' => [
                    ['question' => 'Your child is stuck trying to say a word, blinking and straining. What is the most helpful response?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Wait calmly, keep relaxed eye contact, and let them finish in their own time', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Say the word for them right away to reduce their frustration', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Tell them to slow down and try again', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Look away until they finish speaking', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Waiting calmly without supplying the word or instructing them to slow down avoids adding pressure, which research shows helps rather than filling in or correcting.'],
                    ['question' => 'Why might slowing your own speech rate help a child who stutters?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Children often unconsciously match the pace of people talking with them', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'It teaches the child to speak more quietly', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'It gives the parent time to correct mistakes', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'It has no real effect but feels supportive', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Children tend to mirror the conversational pace of the adults around them, so a calmer, slower adult rate can ease overall time pressure in the exchange.'],
                    ['question' => 'What is a practical way to reduce "time pressure" in daily routines?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Build in a few extra minutes before rushed moments like leaving the house', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Ask the child to speak faster during busy mornings', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Avoid speaking to the child during meals', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Only allow them to talk when there is plenty of silence first', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Reducing rushed, high-demand moments lowers overall time pressure, which many children who stutter respond well to.'],
                ]]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => "A Family's Experience",
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'case_analysis', 'content' => ['body' => "<p>When their son Zaid was three, Farah and Imran noticed he'd started blocking on certain sounds, sometimes going silent mid-word with visible tension in his face. At first they assumed it was a phase, like the word repetitions he'd had at two, and didn't think much of it. But after a couple of months the blocking didn't ease, and Zaid began refusing to answer when asked his name.</p><p>They brought this to a speech-language pathologist, who distinguished what they were seeing from the earlier, typical repetitions: Zaid's blocks, visible tension, and growing avoidance were consistent signs of true stuttering rather than ordinary developmental disfluency. The SLP didn't recommend intensive therapy immediately, but did recommend monitoring closely with periodic check-ins, along with specific coaching for Farah and Imran on reducing time pressure and not finishing his sentences.</p><p>Within several months, some of Zaid's blocking eased, though mild stuttering remained during excitement or tiredness. The family found that naming it calmly at home (\"your words got stuck, that's okay, take your time\") removed much of the shame Zaid had started attaching to it.</p>", 'links' => [['url' => 'https://alzahra.institute/resources', 'reference' => 'Al Zahra Institute Resource Library']]]],
                ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => "Has your child ever shown frustration, avoidance, or embarrassment connected to their speech? How did you or your family respond in the moment?"]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'When to Seek Help',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => "<p>It's reasonable to seek a speech-language pathology evaluation for stuttering any time it worries you, but there are specific signs that make a referral especially worthwhile. These include stuttering that has lasted more than six to twelve months without easing, visible physical tension or struggle, sound or syllable repetitions and prolongations rather than just whole-word or phrase repetitions, a family history of stuttering, and any sign that your child is becoming self-conscious, frustrated, or avoidant about talking.</p><p>An SLP evaluation for stuttering typically involves observing your child's speech in conversation and play, asking about the pattern and history, and sometimes using a standardized severity rating. Depending on what's found, the SLP might recommend simply monitoring with periodic rechecks, direct therapy for the child, or indirect therapy that coaches parents on supportive communication strategies at home, similar to what's covered in this course.</p><p>Early evaluation does not mean early labeling. It means getting clear, individualized guidance so you're not left guessing whether what you're seeing needs more support.</p>"]],
                ['type' => 'assignment', 'content' => ['instructions' => '<p>Over the next week, note any moments your child stutters: what kind of disfluency it was (whole-word repetition vs. sound repetition, prolongation, or block), whether there was visible tension, and whether your child seemed to notice or react to it. Bring your notes to reflect on patterns.</p>', 'files' => [], 'due_date' => null]],
            ],
        ],
    ],
