    'modules' => [
        [
            'title' => 'What the Guidance Actually Says',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => "<p>Major pediatric guidance generally recommends avoiding screen use (other than video calls) for children under 18-24 months, introducing only high-quality, carefully chosen programming with a co-viewing parent between roughly 18 months and 2 years, limiting screen use to about one hour a day of high-quality content for ages 2 to 5, and ensuring consistent limits that don't crowd out sleep, physical activity, and face-to-face interaction for older children.</p><p>These aren't arbitrary numbers. They reflect what we know about how young children actually learn language: through responsive, back-and-forth interaction with real people who respond to what the child does and says in the moment. A screen, however well designed, cannot respond to your specific toddler reaching for something, mispronouncing a word, or asking a follow-up question the way a parent naturally does.</p><p>None of this means occasional or moderate screen use is harmful in itself. It means screens work best as a small, deliberate part of a day built mostly around live interaction, not the default activity filling empty time.</p>"]],
                ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => "How does your family's current screen time compare to the guidance in this module? What feels realistic to adjust, and what feels hard to change given your daily routine?"]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Passive vs. Interactive Screen Use',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => "<p>Not all screen time affects language the same way. The key distinction is between passive viewing, where a child watches content alone with no adult engagement and little response expected, and interactive or co-viewed use, where an adult watches alongside the child, pauses to talk about what's happening, asks questions, and connects the content to the child's own life.</p><p>A toddler left alone in front of fast-moving cartoons for long stretches gets very little language benefit, because the content moves too quickly for a young child to process and there's no one responding to their attempts to react. The same amount of time spent with a parent watching a slower, dialogue-rich children's program together, pausing to ask \"what do you think happens next?\" or naming what a character is feeling, supports vocabulary and comprehension far more.</p><p>Video calls with distant relatives are a notable exception even for very young toddlers, since they involve genuine responsive back-and-forth with a real person, which is closer to live interaction than typical screen content.</p>"]],
                ['type' => 'practical_quiz', 'content' => ['questions' => [
                    ['question' => 'Which is most likely to support a toddler\'s language development?', 'options' => [['text' => 'Watching a slow-paced show together with a parent who talks about it', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'Watching fast-moving cartoons alone for an hour', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Having a tablet on in the background during play', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'Switching between several apps independently', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Co-viewing with an engaged adult who talks about the content turns screen time into an interactive language opportunity, unlike passive solo viewing.'],
                    ['question' => 'Why are video calls with relatives often treated differently from typical screen content for young toddlers?', 'options' => [['text' => 'They involve genuine responsive back-and-forth with a real person', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'They use brighter colors that help attention', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'They are always shorter than other screen activities', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'They have no sound, reducing overstimulation', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Video calls provide real, responsive interaction similar to live conversation, which is why they are treated as an exception even under 18-24 months in most guidance.'],
                    ['question' => 'What generally happens to language input when a toddler watches fast-paced content alone for long stretches?', 'options' => [['text' => 'The language benefit is minimal because there is no responsive interaction', 'correct' => true], ['text' => 'It is equivalent to a parent reading aloud', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'It accelerates vocabulary growth faster than conversation', 'correct' => false], ['text' => 'It has the same effect regardless of pacing or adult involvement', 'correct' => false]], 'explanation' => 'Without a responsive adult and with content too fast to process, passive viewing offers little of the back-and-forth exchange that drives early language learning.'],
                ]]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'A Family Rethinking Their Routine',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'case_study', 'content' => ['body' => "<p>Sana and her husband both worked long hours and relied heavily on a tablet to keep their two-year-old, Yusuf, occupied during meal prep and after dinner, often two to three hours a day of solo cartoon time. When a routine check-up raised a question about Yusuf's relatively small vocabulary for his age, Sana began paying closer attention and realized how much of his day involved screens without anyone talking with him.</p><p>Rather than cutting screens out entirely, which felt unrealistic given their schedule, the family made two specific changes. First, they moved screen time to a single consistent slot, around 45 minutes after dinner, and sat with Yusuf for most of it, pausing to ask simple questions and name what characters were doing. Second, they used meal-prep time, previously tablet time, for parallel talk instead, narrating cooking and letting Yusuf \"help\" with simple tasks while they talked through it.</p><p>Within a couple of months, Sana noticed Yusuf initiating more comments and questions during their shared activities, and the family found the new routine, though it took deliberate effort at first, became their new normal fairly quickly.</p>", 'links' => [['url' => 'https://alzahra.institute/resources', 'reference' => 'Al Zahra Institute Resource Library']]]],
                ['type' => 'discussion', 'content' => ['prompt' => "What specific moments in your day currently rely on screens, and is there one you could realistically convert into a talking-and-doing moment instead, even partially?"]],
            ],
        ],
        [
            'title' => 'Building a Realistic, Language-Rich Routine',
            'blocks' => [
                ['type' => 'reading', 'content' => ['body' => "<p>The goal isn't a screen-free household or perfect compliance with exact hour limits; it's a routine where live interaction is clearly the main event and screens play a smaller, chosen role around it. A few practical habits make this achievable even in busy households.</p><p>Pick a consistent time and duration for screen use rather than letting it happen whenever convenient, which makes it easier to stick to limits and easier for your child to predict and accept. Choose fewer, higher-quality programs over constant channel or app switching, and whenever possible, sit with your child for at least part of it rather than using it purely as independent time. Build in screen-free stretches that are rich in ordinary talk: meals, bath time, car rides, and errands are natural opportunities for conversation that cost nothing extra.</p><p>If screens are currently filling a lot of time out of necessity (work demands, multiple children, exhaustion), be realistic and kind to yourself about change happening gradually, in small shifts, rather than all at once. Any increase in live, responsive interaction helps, even if total screen time doesn't change overnight.</p>"]],
                ['type' => 'assignment', 'content' => ['instructions' => '<p>Track your child\'s screen use for 3 days: how much time, what kind of content, and whether it was solo or with an adult engaging. Pick one specific slot where you could add co-viewing engagement or swap in a talking-and-doing activity, and try it for those 3 days.</p>', 'files' => [], 'due_date' => null]],
